# CS193V exit message. Installed as /home/student/.bash_logout, which bash runs when a
# LOGIN shell exits -- and `cs193v` opens the container with `bash -l`, so this fires on the
# normal way out.
#
# Deliberately does NOT clear the screen, though issue #4 asked for it. A novice's last
# command output is very often the thing they are about to paste to course staff, and
# wiping it on the way out would throw that away. The message plus the prompt and window
# title reverting is an unambiguous enough break.
#
# It will not run if the shell is killed rather than exited. That is acceptable because this
# is cosmetic -- nothing here needs undoing. It is exactly why the rejected background-colour
# idea would have had to live in the launcher instead: a colour left applied after an
# unclean exit would follow a student into unrelated work.
#
# Text lives here rather than in messages.txt because the container cannot see messages.txt;
# only projects/ is mounted.

case $- in
    *i*)
        if [ -t 1 ]; then
            printf '\n  You have left the CS193V development environment. Goodbye!\n\n'
        fi
        ;;
esac
